summary.value:
The paper introduces FlowSearcher, web search framework built on agentic workflow synthesis. It decomposes queries into sub-goals, dynamically constructing adaptive workflow graphs and leveraging hierarchical memory to distill past search experiences. Experiments on GAIA, BrowseComp, and GPQA show that this memory-driven, training-free approach matches or outperforms RLHF-trained systems, highlighting a shift toward deliberative, memory-enhanced web research agents.

The strengths include 1) the proposed method is is well-designed and thoroughly explained; 2) using past experience is interesting; 3) some proposed techniques (e.g. Hierarchical Memory Mechanism) are well motivated; 4) the experimental results look good. However, the reviewer concerns include:
1. Missing ablation and analysis on of incorporating memory (oxdo);
2. No cost/latency analysis (oxdo, kvwa, kcjm, iEBS); 
3. The writing can be improved (kvwa);
4. It is unclear if the hyper-parameter works when transfer the framework to other domains (kcjm);
5. Limited Novelty of Core Components (iEBS);
6. The generalization on new tasks and block synthesis (iEBS);
7. Missing details on the "Instructor" Module (iEBS).

According to the rebuttal, the AC thinks most of the concerns are well addressed, but some are partially addressed, including #5 and #6. #5 is kind of subjective and the rebuttal doesn't provide additional experiments to relieve the generalization concern of #6. Overall, most of the reviewers do agree this paper has some good merits and most of the concerns were well resolved, so the AC would suggest to accept it. The authors are strongly recommended to include the rebuttal materials into the final version and continue to address the novelty/generalization concerns.

